  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 306 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nA baggy slow moving surface low centered over central Illinois\ncontinues to very slowly drift across the region this afternoon\nwith spiraling bands of showers and some embedded thunderstorms\nwrapping around the system. The key forecast question for much \nof the day has been where the heaviest bands of rain would set \nup and begin to pivot this afternoon. The slow moving nature of \nthe system paired with elevated PWATs and warm rain processes \nhas been supportive of rainfall rates in excess of 1-2\"/hr at \ntimes. Even seemingly \"lighter\" radar reflectivity values are \nstill likely producing heavy rainfall. Based on the latest radar\ntrends there are two separate general corridors ongoing at this\nhour. The first extends along the I-80 corridor that is very \ngradually rotating counterclockwise. This is leading to a slight\nnorthwest propagation of the northern periphery into central \nCook and Chicago. It is unclear how far north this reaches over \nthe next few hours but can't rule out heavy downpours and a few \nlightning strikes as far north as downtown (20-30 percent \nchance) through late afternoon. A gradually broadening of those\nband of showers has helped lower the rainfall rates some over \nthe past 15-30 minutes at least. \n\nThe second axis extends from near Pontiac southeast into \ncentral IL/IN but has similarly broadened with localized pockets\nof heavier rates. Earlier concerns of funnel clouds and \npossibly brief landspout have eased for now due to weaker low-\nlevel instability lifting this far northwest compared to what \nmodel guidance had depicted earlier in the day. It will still be\nworth monitoring over the next few hours into portions of \nIroquois, Ford, and Benton Counties, especially if any clearing \noccurs within the broader cloud layer.\n\nA Flash Flood Watch remains in effect until 7 PM CDT for\nCook, DuPage, Will, Grundy, and Livingston Counties to account\nfor the ongoing heavy rainfall though the threat of widespread\nflooding appears to be waning some. This axis should gradually \nshift south and east with time into this evening where the Flash\nFlood Watch remains in effect for all of northwest Indiana and \nIroquois/Kankakee/Ford Counties in Illinois through 1 AM CDT.\n\nPersistent gusty onshore winds will lead to continued high waves\nand dangerous currents along the lakeshore and at Lake Michigan\nbeaches through Monday morning. Stay dry when the waves are\nhigh!\n\nIn the wake of the departing surface low, upper ridging and\nsurface high pressure moves back into the region leading to\nmainly dry conditions for the first part of the upcoming week.\nAn upper level shortwave will move across the Upper Midwest\ntoward midweek which may swing a cold front through the region\nand bring shower and storm chances back to the area. Confidence\nin the details decreases thereafter though additional waves may\nmove within the broader northwest flow at times through the end\nof the week.\n\nPetr",
